@@ -0,0 +1,62 @@
+---
+id: dl-003-json-patch-proto-member-names-are-ordinary-rfc-6902-members
+type: decision-log
+title: "JSON Patch __proto__ member names are ordinary RFC 6902 members"
+status: approved
+---
+
+## Context
+
+dl-002 (still pending/unapproved) decided that `add`, `replace`, `move` and `copy` should refuse any
+target whose last reference token is `__proto__`, reasoning that writing through it onto a plain
+object's inherited accessor would pollute `Object.prototype`. That reasoning does not hold up:
+
+- `parent["__proto__"] = value` invokes the inherited `__proto__` accessor's *setter*, which
+  reassigns `parent`'s own `[[Prototype]]` internal slot. It never touches the actual object bound
+  to the global `Object.prototype`, and it never creates a member on `parent` at all — `value` is
+  simply discarded, and `JSON.stringify(parent)` afterwards no longer even reflects whatever
+  `parent` is supposed to contain if that was its only member. Verified empirically: after
+  `({}).__proto__ = { polluted: true }`, `Object.keys` is `[]`, `JSON.stringify` is `"{}"`, and
+  `({}).polluted` elsewhere is still `undefined`.
+- The one pattern that genuinely reaches the real `Object.prototype` — resolving `target["__proto__"]`
+  as a *read* (which, for a plain object with no own `__proto__` property, yields the actual
+  `Object.prototype`) and then mutating what comes back — was already blocked, independently of the
+  guard, because `resolveToken`/`navigateToParent` require `Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty` to
+  succeed at every intermediate pointer segment, and a plain object never has `__proto__` as an own
+  property.
+
+RFC 6902 does not reserve `__proto__`, or any other member name. Rejecting it was therefore an
+implementation-specific restriction beyond the standard, and it produced exactly the kind of
+surprise the standard doesn't predict: a structurally valid patch, targeting an ordinary (if
+unusually named) object member, failing for a reason the RFC gives no basis for.
+
+## Decision
+
+- `add`, `replace`, `move` and `copy` write object members via `Object.defineProperty(parent, token,
+  { value, writable: true, enumerable: true, configurable: true })` (a shared `setOwnProperty`
+  helper) instead of bracket assignment (`parent[token] = value`). `defineProperty` always creates a
+  genuine own data property regardless of `token`'s value, exactly as `JSON.parse` does when it
+  parses a literal `"__proto__"` key, so a member literally named `__proto__` is now stored, read
+  back, replaced, moved, copied, removed, `JSON.stringify`d, and compared by `test`'s deep-equality
+  exactly like any other member name, through the existing `hasOwnProperty`-gated read/delete paths
+  — no other function needed to change.
+- `guardAgainstPrototypePollution` and its four call sites (in `applyAdd`, `applyReplace`,
+  `applyMove`, `applyCopy`) are removed; there is no longer a special case for `__proto__` as a
+  *target* member name.
+- The existing protection against the dangerous pattern — treating `target["__proto__"]` as a
+  readable/traversable *intermediate* step when it is not an own property — is unchanged, because it
+  was never the guard's doing: it falls out of `resolveToken`/`navigateToParent` already requiring
+  `hasOwnProperty` at every step before returning a value.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- A patch that targets, moves, copies, or removes a member literally named `__proto__` behaves like
+  any other member name, including when the document originates from `JSON.parse` (which creates
+  `__proto__` as an ordinary own property, not a prototype assignment).
+- `Object.prototype` is never reachable for mutation through `applyPatch`: not through this fix (an
+  own-property write never touches it), and not through the pre-existing read-side guard (an
+  intermediate `__proto__` segment still only resolves if it is an own property, which it never is
+  on a plain object whose prototype was set the normal way).
+- This supersedes the `__proto__`-handling portion of dl-002 before dl-002 was ever approved; the
+  rest of dl-002 (root-path behaviour, upfront shape validation, atomicity, deep-cloning patch
+  values) is unaffected and still applies.
