@@ -0,0 +1,77 @@
+---
+id: task-003-audit-applypatch-for-rfc-6902-conformance
+type: task
+title: "Audit applyPatch for RFC 6902 conformance"
+status: approved
+---
+
+## Request
+
+Users of `applyPatch` reported surprising results: some patches they consider invalid are accepted,
+some edge cases give unexpected results, and failures don't always behave the way the RFC reads.
+Review how patches are checked and applied, and make the behaviour conform to RFC 6902 in every
+case, errors included.
+
+## What was done
+
+- Validated the implementation against the RFC 6902 text itself (fetched from
+  rfc-editor.org) and against the community conformance fixtures from
+  `json-patch/json-patch-tests` (`spec_tests.json`'s RFC Appendix A examples, and the broader
+  `tests.json` suite covering array bounds, leading-zero indices, missing `value`/`from`,
+  malformed pointers, unrecognized `op`, escape-sequence edge cases, root-document replace, and
+  copy/move independence). All 108 non-disabled cases from both fixtures passed before any code
+  change, which ruled out the six operations' core add/remove/replace/move/copy/test semantics,
+  array-index/`-`-token handling, and the upfront shape validation as sources of the reported
+  surprises.
+- Found the actual defect in the `__proto__` handling added by task-002/dl-002: `addToParent` and
+  `replaceInParent` wrote object members with plain bracket assignment (`parent[token] = value`),
+  then a `guardAgainstPrototypePollution` check unconditionally rejected any `add`/`replace`/`move`/
+  `copy` whose target's last reference token was `__proto__`. RFC 6902 does not reserve that member
+  name, so rejecting it is a non-conformant extra restriction; and the premise behind it was wrong in
+  two ways once checked empirically against the JS spec:
+  - `parent["__proto__"] = value` never reaches the real `Object.prototype`: it only invokes the
+    inherited `__proto__` accessor's setter, which reassigns `parent`'s own `[[Prototype]]`. The
+    value is not stored as a member at all; `JSON.stringify(parent)` after such an assignment
+    produces `{}`, silently losing the data. So the guard was blocking a case that was already
+    harmless to the rest of the document (and to `Object.prototype` globally) but would have
+    produced a confusing, silently-wrong result for the caller rather than the output RFC 6902
+    expects.
+  - The one genuinely dangerous pattern — reading `target["__proto__"]` as an intermediate pointer
+    segment and then mutating what comes back, which for a literal `{}` is the real
+    `Object.prototype` — was already blocked independently, because `resolveToken`/`navigateToParent`
+    require `hasOwnProperty` on every intermediate step and a plain object never has `__proto__` as
+    an *own* property.
+  - Fix: replaced the bracket assignment in both functions with `Object.defineProperty(parent,
+    token, { value, writable: true, enumerable: true, configurable: true })` (factored into a
+    shared `setOwnProperty` helper) and removed `guardAgainstPrototypePollution` and its four call
+    sites entirely. `defineProperty` always creates a genuine own data property regardless of name,
+    exactly like `JSON.parse` does for a literal `"__proto__"` key, so `"__proto__"` now round-trips
+    like any other member name (readable via `resolveToken`'s existing `hasOwnProperty` check,
+    deletable via `removeFromParent`'s existing `delete`, visible to `Object.keys` in `deepEqual`)
+    with no change needed anywhere else, and with no path to mutating the real `Object.prototype`.
+  - Verified empirically (`node -e`) that bracket assignment leaves `Object.prototype` untouched but
+    drops the data, and that `defineProperty` neither touches `Object.prototype` nor drops the data.
+  - Replaced the old single test ("refuses to add `__proto__`") with six tests in
+    `src/jsonPatch.test.ts`: `add`/`replace`/`move`/`copy`/`remove` all correctly handling a member
+    literally named `__proto__` (including round-tripping through `JSON.stringify`/`JSON.parse` and
+    confirming `Object.prototype` is never polluted), plus a kept characterization test confirming
+    that navigating *through* a non-own `__proto__` as an intermediate pointer segment still fails to
+    resolve (the real guard against the dangerous pattern, which was already correct and unchanged).
+  - Recorded the reasoning and the reversal of the earlier (still-pending, unapproved) `__proto__`
+    decision from dl-002 as
+    dl-003-json-patch-proto-member-names-are-ordinary-rfc-6902-members.
+- Left the rest of dl-002's decisions (root-path behaviour for `remove`/`move`'s `from`, upfront
+  patch-shape validation, clone-based atomicity, deep-cloning values taken from the patch) unchanged:
+  they matched the RFC text, the community test suite, and extensive manual adversarial tracing
+  (array index ABNF incl. leading zeros and `-`, object-vs-array target-type errors, `move`'s
+  proper-prefix restriction, `copy`/`move` independence, pointer escape-sequence ordering, numeric
+  vs. string equality in `test`) with no divergence found.
+
+## Verification
+
+- `npm test` — 57/57 passing (was 52 pre-existing minus the one removed test, plus 6 new tests for
+  the `__proto__` fix).
+- `npm run typecheck` — no errors.
+- Ran the full community conformance suites (`spec_tests.json`, `tests.json` from
+  `json-patch/json-patch-tests`) against `applyPatch` directly: 16/16 and 92/92 of the non-disabled
+  cases pass, both before and after this change.
